course: world
lesson: 22
title: How plants make their food
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Plants make their own food in a process called photosynthesis, which means "putting together with light".
explain: A leaf takes in carbon dioxide gas from the air through tiny holes, and water comes up from the roots through the stem.
explain: Using the energy of sunlight, caught by a green substance called chlorophyll, the leaf turns the water and carbon dioxide into sugar, the plant's food, and gives out oxygen.
explain: So a plant is mostly built from air and water, not from soil: a huge oak tree is made mainly of carbon taken from the air.
explain: The oxygen that plants and tiny sea algae, simple plant-like living things, give out is the oxygen that animals and people breathe.
fact: Photosynthesis is how green plants make their own food.
fact: Plants need light, water and carbon dioxide to make food.
fact: Carbon dioxide enters a leaf through tiny holes in it.
fact: Most leaves are wide and flat so that they catch as much light as possible.
fact: Chlorophyll is the green substance in leaves that catches the energy of light.
fact: The food a plant makes is a kind of sugar.
fact: Photosynthesis gives out oxygen into the air.
fact: Plants also breathe, using some oxygen, all day and night, but in daylight they make much more oxygen than they use.
fact: Plants cannot make food in the dark.
fact: Plants store extra sugar as starch, for example in potatoes and grains.
fact: Most of the mass of a tree comes from carbon dioxide from the air and water, not from the soil.
fact: Tiny algae in the oceans make a large part of the world's oxygen.
fact: All the food animals eat comes, directly or through other animals, from plants and algae.
exercise: What do plants need to make food? | Light, water and carbon dioxide | Soil, oxygen and darkness | Sugar, salt and warm air | Only water and fresh soil
exercise: Which gas do plants take in to make food? | Carbon dioxide | Pure oxygen gas | Nitrogen from the air | Helium, as in balloons
exercise: Which gas do plants give out when they make food? | Oxygen | Carbon dioxide | Smoke | Steam
exercise: What does chlorophyll do? | It catches the energy of light | It pulls water out of the soil | It makes the flowers smell sweet | It keeps the leaves from freezing
exercise: Why are most leaves green? | They contain green chlorophyll | They absorb green light the best | They are full of green-coloured water | They are painted green by the soil
exercise: What kind of food does a plant make? | A kind of sugar | A kind of meat or fat | A kind of salt crystal | A kind of rich soil
exercise: Why can a plant not make food at night? | There is no light for photosynthesis | It has no water at night | Its roots go to sleep | The air has no useful gases in it at night
exercise: Where does most of the material of a big tree come from? | From the air and water | From the soil it grows in | From the insects it traps | From the sunlight itself
exercise: Why are leaves usually wide and flat? | To catch as much light as possible | To hold more rain water in them | To make the plant heavier | To keep the roots in shade
exercise: How does carbon dioxide get into a leaf? | Through tiny holes in the leaf | Up through the roots from soil | Through the petals of flowers | Through the bark of the trunk
exercise: Why is photosynthesis important for animals? | It makes food and oxygen they need | It makes the soil warm in winter | It keeps the rain clouds moving | It turns animals' food into water
exercise: How does a potato plant store its extra food? | As starch in the potato | As oxygen in the leaves | As water in the flowers | As soil around the roots
exercise: Which is true about plants and breathing? | Plants breathe day and night too | Plants never use any oxygen | Plants breathe only at night | Plants breathe only through roots
exercise: Where does the water for photosynthesis come from? | Up from the roots through the stem | In through the petals of the flower | Down from the sky through the leaves | Out of the sugar inside the leaf
test: A plant is kept in a dark cupboard for two weeks. Why does it turn pale and weak? | It cannot make food without light | It gets too much carbon dioxide | Darkness poisons its roots | It makes too much oxygen
test: A boy says a plant gets its food from the soil, like we get food from a plate. Which is right? | The plant makes its own food from light, air and water | The boy is right: plants eat food from the soil with their roots | Plants get their food only from rain | Plants get their food from the wind
test: A scientist grew a willow tree in a pot for five years: the tree gained many kilograms, the soil lost very little. Where did the mass come from? | Mostly from air and water | Mostly from the soil in the pot | Mostly from the pot itself | Mostly from the sunlight's weight
test: Which of these does NOT go into photosynthesis? | Oxygen | Light | Water | Carbon dioxide
test: Why is a sealed glass jar with a plant and a snail in a sunny place good for both of them? | The plant makes oxygen and the snail makes carbon dioxide | The snail makes all the food that the plant needs to grow in the jar | The plant eats the snail's light | The jar makes fresh air for them
test: Why do many plants on a dark forest floor have very large leaves? | To catch the little light that reaches them | To keep the rain off the soil | To hide from the animals | To hold more air inside them, like a balloon
test: What would happen to animals if all plants and algae disappeared? | They would run out of food and oxygen | They would eat soil instead | Nothing, animals make their own food | They would breathe carbon dioxide
test: What does the energy of sunlight do in a leaf? | It powers the making of sugar | It dries the leaf out | It turns into water | It changes the leaf into soil
test: Where do the grains of wheat we eat get their starch from? | From sugar the plant made in its leaves | From starch it took out of the soil | From flour the rain carried down | From insects caught by its roots
test: what do plants need to make food | Light, water and carbon dioxide | Soil, water and oxygen | Only water and soil | Darkness, warmth and oxygen
test: why r leaves green | They have chlorophyll that catches light | They soak up green light to grow | The water inside them is green | They are painted by the minerals in soil
test: a video said plants only breathe at night and not in the day. true? | No, they breathe all the time, but by day they make more oxygen than they use | Yes, they only breathe in the dark, and in the day they just make food instead | Yes, and they make oxygen all night | No, plants never breathe at all
test: Leo reckons a big tree gets most of its weight from the soil. right? | No, most comes from carbon dioxide in the air and water | Yes, the soil goes up into the trunk | Yes, and the hole round a tree gets deeper each year it grows | No, most comes from the sunlight itself
exam: State the substances required by plants for the manufacture of food. | Light, water and carbon dioxide | Soil, oxygen and darkness | Light, oxygen and nitrogen | Water, soil and sugar
exam: Identify the substance within the leaf that captures the energy of light. | Chlorophyll | Stored starch | Dissolved oxygen | Calcium salts
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of the mass of a tree. | It derives chiefly from carbon dioxide of the air and from water | It derives chiefly from the soil | It derives chiefly from sunlight, which is converted directly into wood | It derives chiefly from oxygen of the air
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the respiration of plants? | They respire day and night, but in daylight produce more oxygen than they consume | They respire solely during the night, photosynthesis replacing respiration by day | They do not respire at any time | They respire solely during the day
